WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.959
So I've got something to say about these balls today, and also

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.520
something called

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:08.520
anti-podal points or

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.969
antipodal points. I don't know which way people like to say it,

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:13.750
but I'm just going to call them

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:16.949
antipodal points. The best way you can think about it is on the planet Earth,

00:00:16.949 --> 00:00:20.849
you have to believe the Earth is round, then it has a North and a South Pole.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:23.580
Now those points are

00:00:24.039 --> 00:00:29.999
antipodal (or anti-podal) which means if you draw a line between the North and South Pole

00:00:30.279 --> 00:00:32.609
it'll pass straight through the center of the Earth.

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:38.579
So they're exact opposites on a sphere. But I want to just show you first a map of the world.

00:00:38.579 --> 00:00:43.229
So here what I've got is a flattened out map of the world. So this isn't the world,

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:46.500
this is a flat version of it. Don't get confused between the two,

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:50.520
okay? This is a flat representation of a spherical world.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.610
So North and South is up here, the North and South Pole,

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:59.639
but there's many, many more points we can have. There's the equator right? And so if we picked a point on the equator,

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:02.639
say boom, right here - Indian Ocean.

00:01:03.399 --> 00:01:07.228
We could find out what the antipodal point is. And that would be

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:10.290
180 degrees around.

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:14.700
So for us that would be, we find that we end up here.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:21.390
But also if we got a point here, there's an antipodal point here, or here, or here. Basically, there's an infinite number,

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:28.740
right? But it doesn't have to just be on the equator. There's also antipodal points in different places.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:36.000
Most interestingly you can actually pick different towns or different locations. So we could pick - say here in Auckland,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.479
there's a famous spot for rugby fans, Eden Park,

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:42.210
which is right here. And

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.899
the antipodal point for that is, again, we go back

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:49.470
180 degrees, which we end up somewhere over here.

00:01:50.049 --> 00:01:55.379
But then we have to flip and we go up and then we end up down here in

00:01:56.439 --> 00:01:58.949
southern Spain. So these two points

00:01:59.469 --> 00:02:04.049
are as far away apart as you can be from one another. If I look at my tennis ball,

00:02:04.659 --> 00:02:07.349
there's something interesting. We've got a seam, right?

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:09.819
Now the seam

00:02:09.819 --> 00:02:11.819
actually cuts the

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:13.240
surface

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.240
into two equal areas.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:21.659
That little patch there equals that patch there. So this seam is nice and smooth. It's a loop,

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:24.330
cuts the surface into two equal areas.

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:28.469
But if I just do a little bit of a research here,

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:31.470
look what I find;

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:35.140
antipodal points.

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:37.350
So on the baseball, again

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:44.309
we've got the same situation; two pieces of leather that are exactly the same shape, exactly the same area. And they have this seam,

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:48.300
it's nice and smooth. So it cuts the surface into two equal areas and

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:56.519
yes, I've got my finger on the- on the seam and my thumb. And look, I could rotate it like it's North and South Pole.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:03.929
And then of course a cricket ball, which is sort of the simplest, because it's just got an equator running straight through.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:09.179
So you could pick - you can really run your fingers all around the cricket ball.

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:13.649
And you can find heaps and heaps. But yep, there it goes. This is

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:15.970
special. If

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:23.459
we've got a situation where we have a surface of a sphere, if we have the surface cut into two equal areas

00:03:23.709 --> 00:03:25.630
with a simple loop,

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:29.279
then that loop will contain antipodal points.

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:32.399
And now that, to a mathematician, is

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:39.059
a very exciting proposition. There has to be a reason why that happens. Because I can tell you

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:44.519
any loop which cuts the surface of a sphere into two equal areas

00:03:44.799 --> 00:03:51.809
will always contain antipodal points. This has actually got a really special history for me, because when I was studying

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:57.989
maths at Melbourne Uni, a friend of mine who was just another student, his name is Jan Hi, he was

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:01.979
brilliant at mathematics. And he actually came up with this.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:08.489
So he was just looking at a tennis ball, and sort of looked at the seam and found antipodal points, and he said

00:04:09.579 --> 00:04:15.059
exactly that. And he proved it! And he explained to me the proof. And for me this was like one of the most beautiful experiences,

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:18.899
just someone coming up with maths. So I don't even know if this is a theorem, if it's out there,

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:22.440
I don't really care because it happened to me. It's a personal experience.

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:26.149
So I want to prove this to you right now Brady, okay? Right.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:28.190
We need to have an understanding about antipodal

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:32.839
points to be able to make this work. And I think the best way to do it is looking at something we're familiar with,

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:39.410
which is the world we live on. I've already shown you that the equator has antipodal points by going

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:44.959
180 degrees in the opposite direction. And then also we've got these two towns.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:50.029
So in actual fact, the way you find an antipodal point from wherever you are,

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:51.840
so where you are right now, is

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.690
that you rotate by 180 degrees, and then you flip your location

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:00.170
from - if you're in the northern hemisphere to the southern hemisphere, or vice versa.

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:02.750
So it's the same latitude.

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:10.010
So you basically go 180 degrees and then you use the equator as a mirror. So this here is rotating by

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:13.410
180 degrees, and

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:15.330
then if we take

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:20.479
the same distance here to here, which is taking the mirror image, and then we'll end up in the south of Spain.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:22.849
So that's how we get an antipodal point.

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:25.710
But what if

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:28.130
we took the entire map of the world and

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:34.070
mapped it to its antipodal points? I've got another map of the world,

